Writing Desktop Applications in JavaScript<P>
<HR>
<H1>3  Dialogue boxes and forms</H1>
<HR>
In this chapter !Contacts learns to add and change contacts, in a dialogue box with a field for each detail.
You'll build the third toolkit module, <B>Form</B>, which makes a dialogue box from a list of fields, and use
it for the contact's <I>card</I>. You'll also meet <I>Promises</I>, which let a program wait for someone to
fill in a dialogue box.<P>

<A NAME=S1></A>
<H2>Dialogue boxes</H2>
The first book's windows were built icon by icon: <CODE>{ x: 10, y: 40, w: 200, h: 28, ... }</CODE> for each
one. That was fine for a window with three icons, but the contact's card has thirty. Worse, every dialogue
box should behave the same way, and the Style Guide is specific about how:<P>
<UL>
<LI>Each field has a label to its left.<P>
<LI>The action buttons go at the bottom right. The <I>default</I> button - the one Return presses - is on the
right, and drawn with a thicker border. Cancel is to its left.<P>
<LI>Return in a field moves to the next field; in the last field, it presses the default button.<P>
<LI>Tab and Shift-Tab, and the up and down arrow keys, move between fields.<P>
<LI>Escape is the same as Cancel.<P>
<LI>If something typed isn't acceptable, say so, and put the caret back in that field.<P>
</UL>
Getting all that right once, in a toolkit module, is much better than getting it nearly right in every
dialogue box. So Form takes a list of fields and does the rest:<P>
<PRE>
const form = new Form(task, {
  title: 'Contact',
  fields: [
    { name: 'first', label: 'First name' },
    { name: 'notes', label: 'Notes', lines: 3 },
    { name: 'category', label: 'Category', choices: ['A', 'B'] },
  ],
  buttons: ['Cancel', 'Save'],
  check: (values) =&gt; ...,
});
const values = await form.ask(contact);
</PRE>

<A NAME=S2></A>
<H2>The contact's card</H2>
The contact's dialogue box is in a module called <B>Card</B>. Double-click a contact in !Contacts3 to open it:<P>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/C3Card" WIDTH=750 HEIGHT=473></CENTER>
Here is how Card describes it:<P>
<PRE>
let form = null;                       // made the first time

function makeForm(task) {
  return new Form(task, {
    title: 'Contact',
    help: 'Type the contact\'s details and click Save.',
    fields: [
      { name: 'title', label: 'Title', width: 80 },
      { name: 'first', label: 'First name' },
      { name: 'last', label: 'Surname' },
      { name: 'company', label: 'Company' },
      { name: 'job', label: 'Job title' },
      { name: 'address', label: 'Address', lines: 4 },
      { name: 'postcode', label: 'Postcode', width: 110 },
      { column: 2 },
      { name: 'work', label: 'Work phone', allowed: '0-9 +()-' },
      { name: 'home', label: 'Home phone', allowed: '0-9 +()-' },
      { name: 'mobile', label: 'Mobile', allowed: '0-9 +()-' },
      { name: 'email', label: 'Email' },
      { name: 'web', label: 'Web' },
      { name: 'birthday', label: 'Birthday', width: 120,
        help: 'The birthday as 25/12/1990 (or 1990-12-25).' },
      { name: 'category', label: 'Category', width: 140,
        choices: CATEGORIES },
      { name: 'notes', label: 'Notes', lines: 3 },
    ],
    buttons: ['Cancel', 'Save'],
    check,
  });
}
</PRE>
Each field has a <CODE>name</CODE> (which property of the contact it shows) and a <CODE>label</CODE>, and
perhaps:<P>
<DL>
<DT>width<DD>the width of the field in pixels, if it shouldn't be the usual 220.<P>
<DT>lines<DD>for a field of several lines, such as the address. Form uses the desktop's <B>TextArea</B> for
these: a box of text with a caret, which wraps long lines. (Chapter 17 shows how one works inside.)<P>
<DT>allowed<DD>the characters that may be typed: telephone numbers may only have digits, spaces,
<CODE>+</CODE>, brackets and hyphens. Form gives it to the writable icon as an <CODE>A</CODE> validation
(<I>allowed</I>), a validation string like the <CODE>R5</CODE> border you gave buttons in the first book:
<CODE>A0-9 +()-</CODE> lets through only those characters (<CODE>0-9</CODE> means any digit), and the Wimp
refuses any other key.<P>
<DT>choices<DD>a list to choose from with a pop-up menu, instead of typing.<P>
<DT>option<DD>an on/off option icon (a tick box). The card has none; chapter 9's Choices box uses them.<P>
<DT>help<DD>a line of interactive help for !Help to show.<P>
</DL>
<CODE>{ column: 2 }</CODE> isn't a field: it starts a second column, so the card is wider rather than very
tall.<P>
<CODE>let form = null</CODE> and, further down, <CODE>form ??= makeForm(task)</CODE> make the dialogue box the
first time it's wanted and use the same one after that. <CODE>a ??= b</CODE> is short for <CODE>a = a ?? b</CODE>
(chapter 1): it sets <CODE>form</CODE> only if it's still <CODE>null</CODE>.<P>

<A NAME=S3></A>
<H2>How Form builds a dialogue box</H2>
Form is the longest toolkit module so far, about 190 lines. Its constructor works out where every icon goes, in
two loops. The first makes the fields:<P>
<PRE>
    const top = y;                     // where columns start
    for (const f of opts.fields) {
      if (f.column) { x = right + GAP; y = top; continue; }
      const fx = x + LABEL, fw = f.width ?? FIELD;
      const h = f.lines ? f.lines * 19 + 10 : 28;
      const entry = { spec: f, y, x: fx, w: fw, h };
      if (f.option) {
        icons.push({ x: fx, y, w: fw, h: 28, text: f.label,
          sprite: 'optoff', validation: 'Soptoff,opton',
          button: 'radio', name: f.name, help: f.help });
      } else {
        icons.push({ x, y, w: LABEL - 8, h: 28, text: f.label,
          rjustify: true });
        if (!f.lines) {
          icons.push({ x: fx, y, w: f.choices ? fw - 32 : fw, h: 28,
            text: '', border: true, filled: true,
            bg: f.choices ? 1 : 0, maxLen: f.maxLen ?? 100,
            button: f.choices ? 0 : 'writable',
            validation: f.allowed ? 'A' + f.allowed : '',
            name: f.name, help: f.help });
        }
        if (f.choices) {
          icons.push({ x: fx + fw - 28, y, w: 28, h: 28,
            sprite: 'gright', button: 'click', name: f.name + '!',
            help: 'Click SELECT to choose from a menu.' });
        }
      }
      this.fields.push(entry);
      y += h + 6;
      bottom = Math.max(bottom, y);
      right = Math.max(right, fx + fw);
    }
</PRE>
<CODE>x</CODE> and <CODE>y</CODE> are where the next field goes; each field moves <CODE>y</CODE> down by its
height, and <CODE>{ column: 2 }</CODE> moves <CODE>x</CODE> to the right of everything so far and
<CODE>y</CODE> back to the top. For an ordinary field there are two icons: the label, right-justified so the
labels line up against their fields, and a writable icon. A field with <CODE>choices</CODE> gets a grey,
non-writable icon for the choice and a small button beside it with the sprite that pop-up menus use. A field
with <CODE>lines</CODE> gets only its label here: its TextArea is made after the window.<P>
Each icon is given a <CODE>name</CODE>. The desktop lets you find an icon by its name with
<CODE>w.iconByName</CODE>, so Form never has to count icons.<P>
The second part makes the buttons, from right to left:<P>
<PRE>
    // the buttons, bottom right: the last is the default
    const names = opts.buttons ?? ['Cancel', 'OK'];
    const bw = (b) =&gt; Math.max(80, b.length * 9 + 24);
    const all = names.reduce((t, b) =&gt; t + bw(b) + GAP, GAP);
    right = Math.max(right, all);      // (room for all the buttons)
    let bx = right;
    const by = bottom + GAP;
    for (const b of [...names].reverse()) {
      const w = bw(b), def = b === names.at(-1);
      bx -= w;
      icons.push({ x: bx, y: def ? by - 2 : by, w, h: def ? 40 : 36,
        text: b, border: true, filled: true, hcentre: true,
        button: 'click', validation: def ? 'R6,3' : 'R5,3',
        name: 'button:' + b });
      bx -= GAP;
    }
    const W = right + GAP, H = by + 40 + GAP;
    const win = this.win = task.createWindow({
      title: opts.title ?? 'Form', w: W, h: H, extent: { w: W, h: H },
      flags: { title: true, moveable: true }, returnNext: true,
      icons, help: opts.help,
    });
    // several lines: the desktop's TextArea
    for (const e of this.fields) {
      e.icon = win.iconByName(e.spec.name);
      if (e.spec.lines) {
        e.area = new TextArea(win,
          { x: e.x, y: e.y, w: e.w, h: e.h });
      }
    }
    win.on('click', (ev) =&gt; this.click(ev));
    win.on('key', (ev) =&gt; this.key(ev));
  }
</PRE>
A Form can also be told that some buttons don't close the box, with <CODE>stay: ['Default']</CODE>: such a
button emits a <CODE>'button'</CODE> event instead (the Choices box in chapter 9 needs it).<P>
<CODE>names.at(-1)</CODE> is the last button (<CODE>at</CODE> counts from the end with a minus number), so
the last button in the list is the default, drawn with <CODE>R6</CODE> validation - the thick border - and a
little bigger. The others have <CODE>R5</CODE>, the ordinary action-button border.
<CODE>[...names].reverse()</CODE> reverses a <I>copy</I> of the list (<CODE>reverse</CODE> changes the array
it's used on, and the original is still needed).<P>
The window is made with <CODE>returnNext: true</CODE>. That asks the Wimp to move the caret to the next field
when Return is pressed, and to pass Return on to the program only in the last field. Tab, Shift-Tab and the
arrow keys move between fields anyway, and they move into and out of TextAreas too. "Next" means in reading
order - along each row, then down - so in the card, with its two columns, the caret goes from Title to Work
phone, then First name to Home phone, and so on. (The Category field isn't typed into, so it's skipped.)<P>
Finally each field is matched with its icon, and a TextArea is made for each field with <CODE>lines</CODE>.<P>

<A NAME=S4></A>
<H2>Getting and setting the values</H2>
<PRE>
  // ------------------------------------------------ values
  get() {
    const v = {};
    for (const { spec, icon, area } of this.fields) {
      if (area) v[spec.name] = area.text;
      else if (spec.option) v[spec.name] = icon.selected;
      else v[spec.name] = icon.text.trim();
    }
    return v;
  }
  set(values) {
    for (const { spec, icon, area } of this.fields) {
      const x = values[spec.name];
      if (area) area.text = x ?? '';
      else if (spec.option) icon.setState({ selected: !!x });
      else icon.setText(String(x ?? ''));
    }
  }
</PRE>
<CODE>get()</CODE> reads every field into an object: a TextArea's text, an option icon's on-or-off, or a
writable icon's text with the spaces at either end removed. (<CODE>!!x</CODE> in <CODE>set</CODE> turns a value
into <CODE>true</CODE> or <CODE>false</CODE>, as chapter 2 showed.) <CODE>set(values)</CODE> does the opposite. Both go
through the fields with <CODE>for...of</CODE> and destructuring: <CODE>const { spec, icon, area } of
this.fields</CODE> takes each field entry apart into three variables as it goes.<P>

<A NAME=S5></A>
<H2>Waiting for an answer: Promises</H2>
Opening a dialogue box is easy. The hard part is what comes next: the program has to <I>wait</I> for the person
to click Save or Cancel, and then carry on with what they typed. It mustn't sit in a loop while it waits - as
chapter 9 of the first book explained, the desktop would freeze.<P>
You've used the answer already: <CODE>await</CODE>. In the first book, <CODE>await input('Name? ')</CODE> waited
for a line of typing, and <CODE>await vfs.readText(...)</CODE> for a file. Both worked because
<CODE>input</CODE> and <CODE>readText</CODE> return a <I>Promise</I>: an object that stands for an answer that
isn't ready yet. <CODE>await</CODE> pauses the function until the Promise is <I>resolved</I> - until the answer
arrives - without stopping anything else.<P>
Form makes a Promise of its own:<P>
<PRE>
  // ------------------------------------------------ opening
  /** Open the box: resolves { button, values }, or null (Cancel). */
  run(values = {}) {
    this.set(values);
    const w = this.win;
    w.open({ x: Math.round((wimp.width - w.w) / 2),
      y: Math.round((wimp.height - w.h) / 3), behind: 'top' });
    const first = this.fields.find((e) =&gt; e.icon?.writable);
    if (first) wimp.setCaret(w, first.icon);
    return new Promise((resolve) =&gt; { this.done = resolve; });
  }
  async ask(values) {
    const r = await this.run(values);
    return r ? r.values : null;
  }
  finish(result) {
    this.win.close();
    const done = this.done;
    this.done = null;
    done?.(result);
  }
</PRE>
<CODE>new Promise((resolve) =&gt; { ... })</CODE> makes a Promise, and gives you a function, <CODE>resolve</CODE>,
that you call when the answer is ready. <CODE>run</CODE> opens the box, puts the caret in the first field, and
returns the Promise - but keeps its <CODE>resolve</CODE> function in <CODE>this.done</CODE>. Later, when a button
is pressed, <CODE>finish(result)</CODE> closes the box and calls it, and that is the moment the program's
<CODE>await</CODE> gives its answer.<P>
<CODE>ask</CODE> is the simple version most programs want: it gives just the values, or <CODE>null</CODE> for
Cancel: <CODE>r ? r.values : null</CODE> (the conditional operator, from chapter 1).
<CODE>done?.(result)</CODE> calls <CODE>done</CODE> only if it isn't <CODE>null</CODE>:
<CODE>?.</CODE> is <I>optional chaining</I>, and stops quietly if what's on its left is
<CODE>null</CODE> or <CODE>undefined</CODE>.<P>

<A NAME=S6></A>
<H2>Buttons, checking and keys</H2>
<PRE>
  press(button) {
    if ((this.opts.stay ?? []).includes(button)) {
      this.emit('button', button);
      return;
    }
    if (button === 'Cancel') { this.finish(null); return; }
    const values = this.get();
    const bad = this.opts.check?.(values);
    if (bad) {
      reportError(bad.message);
      const e = this.fields.find((f) =&gt; f.spec.name === bad.field);
      if (e?.area) e.area.focus();
      else if (e?.icon) wimp.setCaret(this.win, e.icon);
      return;
    }
    this.finish({ button, values });
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------ mouse and keys
  click(ev) {
    const name = ev.icon?.name ?? '';
    if (name.startsWith('button:')) this.press(name.slice(7));
    else if (name.endsWith('!')) this.popup(name.slice(0, -1), ev);
    return undefined;
  }
  popup(name, ev) {
    const e = this.fields.find((f) =&gt; f.spec.name === name);
    const menu = new Menu(e.spec.label, e.spec.choices.map((c) =&gt; ({
      text: c, ticked: () =&gt; e.icon.text === c,
      action: () =&gt; e.icon.setText(c),
    })));
    const p = this.win.workToScreen(e.x + e.w, e.y);
    wimp.menus.open(menu, p.x, p.y, { task: this.win.task });
  }
  key(ev) {
    if (ev.code === 27) { this.press('Cancel'); return true; }
    if (ev.code === 13) {                   // Return: the default
      this.press((this.opts.buttons ?? ['OK']).at(-1));
      return true;
    }
    return undefined;
  }
}
</PRE>
<CODE>press</CODE> is called for every button. Cancel finishes with <CODE>null</CODE>. For any other button, it
reads the values and gives them to the <CODE>check</CODE> function, if there is one. If
<CODE>check</CODE> finds a problem, Form reports it, puts the caret in the field it names, and leaves the box
open for the person to put it right. Only when everything's acceptable does the box close.
<CODE>reportError(message)</CODE> is the desktop's error box, which chapter 2 used for its message.<P>
<CODE>click</CODE> tells which icon was clicked by its name. <CODE>name.startsWith('button:')</CODE> and
<CODE>name.endsWith('!')</CODE> are string methods that do what they say; <CODE>name.slice(7)</CODE> is the name
without its first seven characters (<CODE>button:</CODE>), and <CODE>name.slice(0, -1)</CODE> the name without
its last one (a minus number counts from the end).
<CODE>this.fields.find((f) =&gt; ...)</CODE> gives the first field the function says yes to (or
<CODE>undefined</CODE>): it's <CODE>filter</CODE> that stops at the first one. A pop-up button opens a menu of the choices beside
the field, with the current one ticked: <CODE>popup</CODE> makes a Menu, turns the field's top-right corner
from work-area to screen coordinates with <CODE>workToScreen</CODE> (menus are placed on the screen, windows'
contents in their work area), and opens it there with <CODE>wimp.menus.open(menu, x, y, { task })</CODE> - the
same thing that happens, at the pointer, when you click Menu.<P>
<CODE>key</CODE> presses Cancel for Escape, and the default button for Return. (Return only reaches it from the
last field, because of <CODE>returnNext</CODE>. A TextArea keeps Return for itself, to start a new line.)<P>

<A NAME=S7></A>
<H2>Checking a contact</H2>
Card's <CODE>check</CODE> function is the rules for a contact:<P>
<PRE>
/** Is everything filled in properly? null, or what's wrong. */
function check(v) {
  if (!v.first &amp;&amp; !v.last &amp;&amp; !v.company) {
    return { field: 'first', message: 'Please give a name' };
  }
  if (v.email &amp;&amp; !/^[^@\s]+@[^@\s]+\.[^@\s]+$/.test(v.email)) {
    return { field: 'email', message: 'That email address is wrong' };
  }
  if (v.birthday &amp;&amp; !dateKey(v.birthday)) {
    return { field: 'birthday',
      message: 'Give the birthday as 25/12/1990' };
  }
  return null;
}

/** '25/12/1990' or '1990-12-25' as '1990-12-25' (or null). */
export function dateKey(s) {
  let m = /^(\d{1,2})\/(\d{1,2})\/(\d{4})$/.exec(s);
  const [y, mo, d] = m ? [m[3], m[2], m[1]]
    : (m = /^(\d{4})-(\d\d)-(\d\d)$/.exec(s)) ? [m[1], m[2], m[3]]
    : [];
  if (!y) return null;
  const date = new Date(+y, mo - 1, +d);
  if (date.getMonth() !== mo - 1) return null;       // 31/02 ...
  return `${y}-${String(mo).padStart(2, '0')}-${String(d)
    .padStart(2, '0')}`;
}
</PRE>
It needs a name or a company, an email address that looks like one, and a birthday that's a real date.<P>
The email address is checked with a <I>regular expression</I>, a pattern for text, written between slashes
(the first book used one, <CODE>/\s+/</CODE>, to split words). In <CODE>/^[^@\s]+@[^@\s]+\.[^@\s]+$/</CODE>:
<CODE>^</CODE> and <CODE>$</CODE> mean the start and end of the text; <CODE>[^@\s]</CODE> is any one character
that isn't <CODE>@</CODE> or a space (<CODE>\s</CODE> is any space; <CODE>^</CODE> inside brackets means
"not"); <CODE>+</CODE> means one or more of them; <CODE>\.</CODE> is a real dot (a plain <CODE>.</CODE> would
match anything). So: something, <CODE>@</CODE>, something, a dot, something - with no spaces or second
<CODE>@</CODE>. <CODE>test</CODE> says whether the text matches.<P>
Birthdays can be typed the British way, <I>25/12/1990</I>, or as <I>1990-12-25</I>, and <CODE>dateKey</CODE>
turns either into the second form, which is the one !Contacts keeps (it sorts properly, and in chapter 11 the
organiser uses the same form for every date). The two patterns are
<CODE>/^(\d{1,2})\/(\d{1,2})\/(\d{4})$/</CODE> (one or two digits, a slash, one or two digits, a slash, four
digits: <CODE>\d</CODE> is a digit, <CODE>{1,2}</CODE> means one or two of it, and <CODE>\/</CODE> is a
slash, which needs a backslash inside a pattern written between slashes) and
<CODE>/^(\d{4})-(\d\d)-(\d\d)$/</CODE>. <CODE>exec</CODE> is the regular-expression method that gives the
parts in round brackets as <CODE>m[1]</CODE>, <CODE>m[2]</CODE> and so on, or <CODE>null</CODE> if the text
doesn't match. Then <CODE>const [y, mo, d] = ...</CODE> destructures an <I>array</I>, taking its first three
items into three variables. The conditional operators choose which array: day-month-year for the first form,
year-month-day for the second, or an empty one if neither matched. In the middle one,
<CODE>(m = /.../.exec(s))</CODE> is an assignment used as a value: an assignment gives the value it assigns,
so this sets <CODE>m</CODE> and tests it at the same time.<P>
The last line puts the date back together with <CODE>padStart(2, '0')</CODE>, which pads a string on the
left to two characters with zeros, so 7 becomes 07.<P>
The last check is a neat trick. <CODE>new Date(year, month, day)</CODE> makes a date - but JavaScript counts
months from 0, so January is 0 and December 11, which is why the code passes <CODE>mo - 1</CODE> (and
<CODE>+y</CODE> turns the text <CODE>'1990'</CODE> into a number). <CODE>new Date(1990, 1, 31)</CODE> - the 31st
of February - is quietly turned into the 3rd of March, so if the month has changed, the date wasn't real.
(Chapter 11 has more to say about dates.)<P>

<A NAME=S8></A>
<H2>Adding and changing contacts</H2>
<CODE>editCard</CODE>, at the end of Card, opens the box with a contact's values and a title, and gives back the
new values (with the birthday tidied) or <CODE>null</CODE>. Its parameter <CODE>contact = {}</CODE> has a
<I>default value</I>, as the ListView's <CODE>key</CODE> did in chapter 2: if <CODE>editCard</CODE> is called without a contact (as it is for a new one), it gets an
empty object instead of <CODE>undefined</CODE>. MainWindow uses it:<P>
<PRE>
  // ------------------------------------------------ changes
  /** Give the window the keyboard again (after a dialogue box). */
  focus() { if (this.win.isOpen) wimp.setCaret(this.win); }

  async add() {
    const v = await editCard(this.task, {}, 'New contact');
    this.focus();
    if (!v) return;
    const c = this.book.add(v);
    this.refresh();
    this.list.select(c);
    this.list.ensureVisible(c);
  }
  async edit(c = this.list.selected()[0]) {
    if (!c) return;
    const v = await editCard(this.task, c, nameOf(c));
    this.focus();
    if (!v) return;
    this.book.update(c.id, v);
    this.refresh();
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------ the menu
  openMenu(ev) {
    wimp.menus.openAt(this.menu(), ev, { task: this.task,
      onClose: () =&gt; this.list.endTemporary() });
  }
  menu() {
    return new Menu('Contacts', [
      { text: 'New contact', action: () =&gt; this.add() },
      { text: 'Change', action: () =&gt; this.edit() },
    ]);
  }
</PRE>
<CODE>add</CODE> and <CODE>edit</CODE> are <CODE>async</CODE> methods, because they <CODE>await</CODE> the card.
After it closes, <CODE>focus</CODE> gives the list window the keyboard back, so the arrow keys work again
straight away. A new contact is added to the Book, selected and scrolled into view; a changed one is given
to the Book's new <CODE>update</CODE> method:<P>
<PRE>
  update(id, fields) {
    const c = { ...this.contacts.get(id), ...fields, id };
    this.contacts.set(id, c);
    this.emit('change', { updated: c });
    return c;
  }
</PRE>
<CODE>{ ...this.contacts.get(id), ...fields, id }</CODE> builds a <I>new</I> contact: all the old one's
properties, then the new values on top (a later property of the same name wins), then the id. The old object
isn't changed. That matters: the card was given the old contact to show, and until Save is clicked, nothing
the person types should touch the address book. Cancel simply throws the typing away.<P>
Model also gains <CODE>CATEGORIES</CODE>, the list the card's Category pop-up offers, and Check a test for
<CODE>update</CODE>.<P>
Both methods call <CODE>refresh()</CODE> so the list shows the change. That's a little clumsy - every piece of
code that changes the Book has to remember to do it - and chapter 4 has a better way.<P>

<A NAME=S9></A>
<H2>The menu</H2>
Click Menu on the list: there's a small menu with <I>New contact</I> and <I>Change</I>. The ListView's
<CODE>menu</CODE> event opens it, and the menu is built afresh each time it opens, as in the first book.<P>
Try clicking Menu over a contact that isn't selected. It's selected while the menu is open (the Style Guide's
temporary selection), so <I>Change</I> changes that one; when the menu closes without being used,
<CODE>onClose</CODE> calls the list's <CODE>endTemporary</CODE> to deselect it again.<P>
To try the card:<P>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Run !Contacts3 and double-click a contact (or select it and press Return).<P>
<DT><DD>2  Change the telephone number, and press Return a few times: the caret moves from field to field,
across the two columns and down.<P>
<DT><DD>3  Type a wrong email address, such as <CODE>ann.example.com</CODE>, and click Save. The box stays
open, and the caret is in the Email field.<P>
<DT><DD>4  Put it right and click Save. The list shows the change.<P>
<DT><DD>5  Choose <I>Menu &gt; New contact</I>, fill in a name, and press Escape: nothing is added.<P>
</DL>

<A NAME=S10></A>
<H2>Summary</H2>
<UL>
<LI>The Style Guide's dialogue boxes: labels on the left, the default button bottom right with a thick border,
Cancel to its left, Return to move on (and in the last field to press the default), Escape to cancel.<P>
<LI>Form (the toolkit) builds a dialogue box from a list of fields; <CODE>ask()</CODE> gives the values or
<CODE>null</CODE>; a <CODE>check</CODE> function keeps the box open until the values are acceptable.<P>
<LI>A Promise stands for an answer that isn't ready yet; <CODE>new Promise((resolve) =&gt; ...)</CODE> makes one,
and calling <CODE>resolve</CODE> gives <CODE>await</CODE> its answer.<P>
<LI>Edit a copy: the dialogue box works on its own values, and the model is changed only when the person
confirms.<P>
<LI>New JavaScript: <CODE>a ??= b</CODE>, <CODE>obj?.x</CODE> and <CODE>fn?.()</CODE>,
<CODE>at(-1)</CODE>, <CODE>startsWith</CODE>, <CODE>endsWith</CODE>, <CODE>padStart</CODE>, regular
expressions, <CODE>exec</CODE>, array destructuring, months counted from 0.<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S11></A>
<H2>Exercises</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Add a field for a nickname to the card, after the surname.<P>
<DT><DD>2  Make the card refuse a telephone number with fewer than six digits.<P>
<DT><DD>3  Add a menu item <I>Delete</I> that removes the selected contacts from the list. (The Book has no way to
remove a contact yet: add a <CODE>remove(id)</CODE> method that deletes it from the Map and emits
<CODE>change</CODE>.)<P>
<DT><DD>4  What would happen if <CODE>Book.update</CODE> changed the old contact object instead of making a new
one, and why might that matter when the card is open?<P>
</DL>

<A NAME=S12></A>
<H2>Answers</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  In Card's list of fields, after the surname:
<PRE>
{ name: 'nickname', label: 'Nickname' },
</PRE>
<DT><DD>2  In <CODE>check</CODE>:
<PRE>
for (const f of ['work', 'home', 'mobile']) {
  const digits = (v[f] ?? '').replace(/\D/g, '');
  if (v[f] &amp;&amp; digits.length &lt; 6) {
    return { field: f, message: 'That number is too short' };
  }
}
</PRE>
(<CODE>\D</CODE> matches anything that isn't a digit, so removing them leaves just the digits.)
<DT><DD>3  In the Book:
<PRE>
remove(id) {
  this.contacts.delete(id);
  this.emit('change', { removed: id });
}
</PRE>
and in MainWindow's menu:
<PRE>
{ text: 'Delete', action: () =&gt; {
  for (const c of this.list.selected()) this.book.remove(c.id);
  this.refresh();
} },
</PRE>
Chapter 4 does this properly, with shading and a question first.
<DT><DD>4  Anything else holding the old object - the list's items, or a card still open on it - would see the
change at once, before it was meant to, and it couldn't be undone by throwing the new values away. Making a new
object keeps the old one exactly as it was, which chapter 9's Undo relies on.<P>
</DL>

